<https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/claim/morphoeval-nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Wiggs et al. (1997), Li et al. (2011) & Eskandari et al. (2015) cylindrical bilayer mucosal folding, period-doubling & Poiseuille airway resistance, coupled to a Weibel (1963) bronchial-tree impedance network with Venegas (2005) flow-stretch feedback (Mammalian bronchial airway mucosa & submucosa (Bos taurus, Homo sapiens, Ovis aries); human Weibel model-A conducting tree)",
        "Wiggs et al. (1997), Li et al. (2011) & Eskandari et al. (2015) cylindrical bilayer mucosal folding, period-doubling & Poiseuille airway resistance, coupled to a Weibel (1963) bronchial-tree impedance network with Venegas (2005) flow-stretch feedback faithfully reproduces Mammalian bronchial airway mucosa & submucosa (Bos taurus, Homo sapiens, Ovis aries); human Weibel model-A conducting tree morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/probknow/resource/math-law/nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing-5> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) 2018-nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Mammalian bronchial airway mucosa & submucosa (Bos taurus, Homo sapiens, Ovis aries); human Weibel model-A conducting tree specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is the weight of evidence (MorphoEval 0.1.0 overall 84.3/100, audit 2026-10-19-airway-mucosal-creasing-audit-v3) and the assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/8a7928d7/2018-nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing.py> ;
    schema:about "Mammalian bronchial airway mucosa & submucosa (Bos taurus, Homo sapiens, Ovis aries); human Weibel model-A conducting tree" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: A finite-element solution of the cylindrical bilayer, Horsfield asymmetric geometry, acinar tissue-stretch coupling, gas-exchange or pressure-volume dynamics, a quantitative reproduction of Venegas et al. (2005) PET images, or a validated pressure-controlled (fixed driving pressure) mode",
        "reconstructed_in_script: 12-point three-species wavenumber collapse against a vendored table; polar star-contour segmentation of one SHA-256-pinned bronchiole micrograph",
        "reconstructed_in_script: An L-BFGS-B ring energy relaxation that is used for the lumen profile only in part (75 % analytic template + 25 % minimiser) and whose A/A_0 and period-doubling outputs are 88 %/85 % the passed-in reference values by default",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Cylindrical bilayer wavenumber law with a fitted finite-thickness factor and wall-coupling term; inextensible-perimeter lumen-area occlusion with a closed-form period-doubling ratio; Hagen-Poiseuille (A_0/A)^2 amplification",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Weibel G0-G12 tree built from a SHA-256-pinned JSON, exact bottom-up series-parallel subtree impedance and top-down flow split, per-branch log-normal ASM strain, generation-independent h_m/R_0 and m*, saturating one-sided Venegas feedback under-relaxed to a fixed point, defect fraction / ventilation CV / run-length clustering / whole-tree resistance ratio, kappa x phenotype x seed sweep, analytic Weibel G0-G16 small-airway resistance share",
        "reported_in_source: Jin et al. (2011): creases in growing soft tissue (sulcal self-contact)",
        "reported_in_source: Li et al. (2011) / Eskandari et al. (2015): cylindrical bilayer circumferential mode selection m* ~ (R_0/h_m)(3 mu_s/mu_m)^(1/3), inverse mucosal-thickness scaling, period-doubling and creasing with growth",
        "reported_in_source: Macklem & Mead (1967): airways < 2 mm contribute only ~10-20 % of total airway resistance",
        "reported_in_source: Venegas et al. (2005): PET ventilation defects in bronchoconstricted asthmatics are patchy and self-organised (~30 % of lung volume) via a flow-stretch feedback on airway smooth muscle on a bifurcating tree",
        "reported_in_source: Weibel (1963): symmetric dichotomous model-A geometry (generation-wise diameters and lengths) of the human conducting airways",
        "reported_in_source: Wiggs et al. (1997): mucosal folding of a thin stiff epithelium on a compliant submucosa under ASM shortening; folded lumen area and Hagen-Poiseuille resistance amplification (~4.5x normal, >15x in severe asthma)",
        "transfer_boundary: The 3D coordinates of the tree (35 deg half-angle, 90 deg plane rotation per generation) are for rendering only and do not enter the impedance network",
        "transfer_boundary: The tree is a rigid laminar Poiseuille network: no Pedley (1970) entrance-flow correction, wall compliance, inertance or upper-airway resistance, so the pure-Poiseuille small-airway share (0.43) is 2-4x the measured Macklem & Mead (1967) 0.10-0.20 and is reported as a known limitation",
        "transfer_boundary: sigma = 0.10 is assumed (no offline measurement); kappa = 0.2 is the largest value of a coarse grid keeping the healthy tree below 5 % defects, so only the asthma and bronchitis defect fractions are predictions" ;
    pk:aristotelianEfficientCause "7. Elastic Shell/Sheet Buckling" ;
    pk:aristotelianMegistonGenos "I. Enaima (Vertebrata)" ;
    pk:aristotelianPoionForm "Gyri, Villi, Loops & Snap-Shells" ;
    pk:aristotelianPosonLaw "Wrinkling lambda ~ h(E_f/E_s)^1/3" ;
    pk:aristotelianSubalternClass "Mammalia (Zootoka)" ;
    pk:biologicalGenus "Bos" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/8a7928d7/2018-nelson-airway-mucosal-creasing.py; output: docs/media/airway_mucosal_creasing_comparison.png; organism: Mammalian bronchial airway mucosa & submucosa (Bos taurus, Homo sapiens, Ovis aries); human Weibel model-A conducting tree; checkpoint commit: https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/commit/8a7928d7; environment: python 3.14.7, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.16.3, matplotlib 3.10.7, Pillow 12.0.0; spec_fingerprint: b6099e6eb43c410229d8bb8eb684966c04bd0b7fa64bc5000b8d74de4532f720; artifact_sha256: 3315968b82d9c2f6bc09c306aa6191caee6e54bdf97a1073c2a35e80d95f4f00; input_dataset_sha256: {datasets/airway_mucosal_creasing/airway_mucosal_creasing_reference.json=de50bdef15ba022d..., datasets/airway_mucosal_creasing/bronchial_tree_impedance_reference.json=98da4936e1caeb8a..., datasets/airway_mucosal_creasing/bronchiole_mucosal_rosette_specimen.jpg=ecdf9c611a8c6f1c..., datasets/airway_mucosal_creasing/paper_citations.bib=dc5cda198aa48e19..., datasets/airway_mucosal_creasing/pass3_citations.bib=02b11e335d715fbb..., datasets/airway_mucosal_creasing/scout_manifest.json=c73a19386245eae3...}; supersedes_audit_id: 2026-10-19-airway-mucosal-creasing-audit-v2; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Wiggs et al. (1997): mucosal folding of a thin stiff epithelium on a compliant submucosa under ASM shortening; folded lumen area and Hagen-Poiseuille resistance amplification (~4.5x normal, >15x in severe asthma) | Li et al. (2011) / Eskandari et al. (2015): cylindrical bilayer circumferential mode selection m* ~ (R_0/h_m)(3 mu_s/mu_m)^(1/3), inverse mucosal-thickness scaling, period-doubling and creasing with growth | Jin et al. (2011): creases in growing soft tissue (sulcal self-contact) | Weibel (1963): symmetric dichotomous model-A geometry (generation-wise diameters and lengths) of the human conducting airways | Venegas et al. (2005): PET ventilation defects in bronchoconstricted asthmatics are patchy and self-organised (~30 % of lung volume) via a flow-stretch feedback on airway smooth muscle on a bifurcating tree | Macklem & Mead (1967): airways < 2 mm contribute only ~10-20 % of total airway resistance; reconstructed_in_script: Cylindrical bilayer wavenumber law with a fitted finite-thickness factor and wall-coupling term; inextensible-perimeter lumen-area occlusion with a closed-form period-doubling ratio; Hagen-Poiseuille (A_0/A)^2 amplification | An L-BFGS-B ring energy relaxation that is used for the lumen profile only in part (75 % analytic template + 25 % minimiser) and whose A/A_0 and period-doubling outputs are 88 %/85 % the passed-in reference values by default | 12-point three-species wavenumber collapse against a vendored table; polar star-contour segmentation of one SHA-256-pinned bronchiole micrograph | Weibel G0-G12 tree built from a SHA-256-pinned JSON, exact bottom-up series-parallel subtree impedance and top-down flow split, per-branch log-normal ASM strain, generation-independent h_m/R_0 and m*, saturating one-sided Venegas feedback under-relaxed to a fixed point, defect fraction / ventilation CV / run-length clustering / whole-tree resistance ratio, kappa x phenotype x seed sweep, analytic Weibel G0-G16 small-airway resistance share; transfer_boundary: The tree is a rigid laminar Poiseuille network: no Pedley (1970) entrance-flow correction, wall compliance, inertance or upper-airway resistance, so the pure-Poiseuille small-airway share (0.43) is 2-4x the measured Macklem & Mead (1967) 0.10-0.20 and is reported as a known limitation | sigma = 0.10 is assumed (no offline measurement); kappa = 0.2 is the largest value of a coarse grid keeping the healthy tree below 5 % defects, so only the asthma and bronchitis defect fractions are predictions | The 3D coordinates of the tree (35 deg half-angle, 90 deg plane rotation per generation) are for rendering only and do not enter the impedance network; does_not_claim: A finite-element solution of the cylindrical bilayer, Horsfield asymmetric geometry, acinar tissue-stretch coupling, gas-exchange or pressure-volume dynamics, a quantitative reproduction of Venegas et al. (2005) PET images, or a validated pressure-controlled (fixed driving pressure) mode]" ;
